    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The western Grand Canyon is an excellent natural laboratory for studying normal faulting processes. In this study we have documented fault and fold geometry for a ~10-km long section of the frogy fault system using differential GPS surveying of beds in the Upper Esplanade fm. Normal Faults in the study area have consistently steep dips, typically > 70o and are independent of both depth and rock type. Both hanging wall and footwall are folded. Hanging wall beds roll gently into the frogy fault, while footwall beds are tilted away from the fault. Hanging wall fold width is independent of throw. Maximum bedding dip, however, is both a function of fault throw and the presence of synthetic or antithetic faults. Increasing fault throw and synthetic faults both act to increase bedding dip while antithetic faults act to decrease bed dips. We illustrate that these observations are consistent with simple mechanical models and may help explain why observed structures deviate from elastic models that do not incorporate secondary structures.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
